# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# mantle-kr-herald — environment
#
# Every line is tagged so you can see at a glance what you actually have to set:
#
#   [REQUIRED]  the command will not run without it
#   [OPTIONAL]  has a working default, or the feature is simply off without it
#   [PICK ONE]  one of a set — see the block's note
#
# "Required" is not one thing, though: it depends on where you are running and in
# which storage mode. Those two would make four combinations, except a hosted
# deployment must be `cloud` (with `local` it writes approved documents to the
# function's own ephemeral filesystem — `api/[...path].ts` refuses to start rather
# than serve that). So there are three profiles, and each variable below belongs to
# one or more of them:
#
#                                     A       B       C
#   A = pnpm serve, local mode      serve   serve   Vercel
#   B = pnpm serve, cloud mode      local   cloud   (cloud)
#   C = the Vercel deployment      ─────── ─────── ───────
#   DATABASE_URL                      ●       ●       ●
#   HERALD_DB_ENV                     ●       ●       ●
#   HERALD_STORAGE_MODE               ●       ●       ● (must be "cloud")
#   HERALD_AUTH_USERNAME              ●       ●       ●
#   HERALD_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH         ●       ●       ●
#   HERALD_SESSION_SECRET             ●       ●       ●
#   HERALD_DEPLOYMENT_ORIGIN          ·       ·       ●
#   HERALD_TRUST_PROXY                ·       ·       ● (must be "true")
#   HERALD_TRUST_PROXY_HOPS           ·       ·       ○
#   HERALD_SENDS_ENABLED              ·       ·       ○
#   PORT                              ○       ○       ·  (the platform assigns it)
#   GOOGLE_* / GOOGLE_SA_KEY_FILE     ·       ●       ●  §3a, [PICK ONE]
#   GDRIVE_REVIEW_FOLDER_ID           ·       ●       ●
#   GDRIVE_APPROVED_FOLDER_ID         ·       ●       ●
#   every other GDRIVE_* / GSHEET_*   ·       ○       ○
#   LARK_DRIVE_* / LARK_WORKSPACE_URL ·       ○       ○
#   §2 collection (TWITTERAPI_IO_KEY,
#      REFERENCE_X_HANDLE, LARK_APP_*,
#      LARK_CHAT_IDS, LARK_BASE_URL)   per-command — the deployment never collects
#   HERALD_COLLECT_MAX_PAGES (§2)     ·       ·       ·  only a one-off GAP backfill; pnpm collect[:reference] only
#   §4 delivery (TELEGRAM_*,
#      TYPEFULLY_*, X_PREMIUM)         per-command — needed wherever a send runs
#   HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR (§1)            ·       ·       ·  only the scheduler units set it
#   HERALD_TRANSLATE_SINCE (§1)       ·       ·       ·  only pnpm watch's systemd unit sets it
#   HERALD_WATCH_BATCH (§1)           ·       ·       ·  pnpm watch's systemd unit can set it
#                                                        (ships commented out — default 3 applies)
#   HERALD_CONVERT_BATCH (§1)         ·       ·       ·  pnpm convert:tick's systemd unit can set it
#                                                        (ships commented out — default 1 applies)
#   HERALD_DEPLOY_DIR (§1)            ·       ·       ·  pnpm doctor only, and only on a machine that
#                                                        has a second, deployed checkout
#   HERALD_SMOKE_* (§6a)              ·       ·       ·  pnpm deploy:smoke / pnpm creds:check only
#                                                        (run on YOUR machine — never set on Vercel)
#
#   ● required   ○ optional   · not read at all
#
# Only the ● rows in the top block are refusals you find out about at startup. The
# §3 ● rows degrade silently instead: `createDeps` probes that config in a try/catch
# and simply drops the target it cannot build, so a cloud install missing them boots,
# serves, and quietly offers local-only publishing. §6 has the full account.
#
# Nothing here is needed for the translate / convert / format stages: those never
# call an external API. Setup procedures live in docs/ko/setup/ — this file only
# names the variables. Check your setup with `pnpm doctor`.
#
# Three documents, three different questions — reach for the one that matches yours:
#   this file          what each variable IS, and how to obtain a value for it
#   docs/ko/env.md     what BREAKS if you leave it out or get it wrong, and where
#                      it stops. Written symptom-first, so start there when
#                      something is already failing and you do not yet know which
#                      variable is to blame
#   docs/ko/setup/     the click-by-click procedure for issuing a credential
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═══ 1. Always ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# [REQUIRED] Where approved artifacts are preserved.
#
# **Start with `local`, and switch to `cloud` once your setup is finished.**
# That is the intended path, not a fallback: in local mode the whole pipeline
# runs — collect, translate, convert, format, review — with no cloud credentials
# at all. Section 3 below stays empty until you are ready. When your Google
# Drive credentials are set and `pnpm doctor` is green, change this one line to
# `cloud` (Lark Drive and the Google Sheet are optional — doctor only warns).
#
#   local — everything stays under output/, including the published documents
#           (output/publish/local/{review,approved}/). **You own that tree**: it
#           is git-ignored, so nothing backs it up for you, and neither
#           `pnpm archive` nor `pnpm clean` bounds it — both leave
#           output/publish/local/ alone (they only touch worksheets and
#           expired output/archive/ folders). Know what lives where
#           (docs/ko/artifacts.md) and prune or back it up yourself.
#   cloud — Google/Lark Drive becomes the record of truth; drive:publish uploads
#           there, the Sheet commands run, and section 3 becomes required.
#           output/ is still your working tree — the Drive copy is the record,
#           not a backup of it.
#
# Never inferred. An unset value fails loudly rather than guessing, because
# guessing wrong sends published work to the wrong place: a cloud operator would
# find their documents sitting in output/ instead of Drive, with drive:publish
# reporting success either way.
#
# Promotion steps: docs/ko/quickstart.md §5.
HERALD_STORAGE_MODE=local

# [REQUIRED] The Postgres connection string that backs the pipeline's record of
# truth — translations, conversions, renderings, deliveries, and every other
# ledger that used to live only under output/ as JSON. A standard libpq URL,
# e.g. postgres://user:pass@host:5432/herald.
#
# No database yet? One container is the fastest way to a working one, and the
# URL it produces is the second line below (docs/ko/quickstart.md §1.5):
#
#   docker run -d --name herald-db \
#     -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=herald -e POSTGRES_DB=herald \
#     -p 5432:5432 postgres:16-alpine
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:herald@127.0.0.1:5432/herald
#
# Required in local storage mode too. The two "local"s are different axes: the
# storage mode decides where PUBLISHED artifacts go (output/ vs Drive), while the
# ledgers live in Postgres in either mode. Do not create the tables by hand —
# `pnpm serve`, `pnpm db:import` and `pnpm db:export` apply the schema
# idempotently on startup. `pnpm doctor` and `pnpm status` deliberately do not,
# so on a brand-new database run `pnpm db:import --yes` once first.
DATABASE_URL=

# [REQUIRED] Which database DATABASE_URL points at: production | development.
#
# Never inferred from the URL. A connection string looks exactly the same
# whether it points at your laptop's scratch database or the team's shared
# one, so guessing from it — or defaulting when this is unset — is the one
# failure this must not allow: a wrong guess either strands a day of approvals
# in a database nobody reads, or puts a local experiment on the team's live
# board. You state which one this is; nothing derives it for you.
HERALD_DB_ENV=

# [OPTIONAL] Overrides where the whole output/ tree lives — worksheets,
# pending.json, the collect watermark, everything `src/paths.ts`'s OUTPUT_DIR
# derives paths from. Nobody sets this by hand in normal use: the two scheduler
# units that write artifacts — `deploy/herald-watch.service` (`pnpm watch`) and
# `deploy/herald-convert.service` (`pnpm convert:tick`) — are the only places
# that do, both pointing it at the same `%h/.herald/output` so the scheduler's own
# artifact tree never shares the file-backed collect watermark (output/x/state.json
# — see src/cli/stores.ts) or `output/variants/pending.json` with a hand-run local
# command. Resolved to an absolute path
# no matter what you set it to. Leave unset for the ordinary repo-relative
# output/ every other command uses; `pnpm doctor` reports whichever root is
# actually in effect.
HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR=

# [OPTIONAL] Read only by `pnpm watch`: the oldest post the scheduler will ever
# translate, passed straight through to `translate:prepare --since`. Set on the
# same systemd unit as HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR above, and for the same reason — it is a
# scheduler-only concern.
#
# Why it exists: `translate:prepare` takes the first `--limit` items of the whole
# untranslated set, oldest first. Against production on 2026-08-06 that was 211
# items reaching back to 2026-06-01, so a scheduler doing 3 items every two hours
# would have spent ~6 days on history before touching the 23 threads its own
# first tick had just collected. The cutoff is a content decision — which old
# posts the Korean account is choosing never to translate — so it lives here and
# not in code.
#
# Any ISO-8601 value; normalised to UTC before use. Unparseable values are
# refused at startup rather than silently filtering the batch to nothing. Leave
# unset to keep the whole-backlog behaviour a hand-run `pnpm watch` gets.
HERALD_TRANSLATE_SINCE=

# [OPTIONAL] Read only by `pnpm watch`: how many items each tick hands to both
# `translate:prepare --limit` and `translate:align --limit`. Set on the same
# systemd unit as HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR and HERALD_TRANSLATE_SINCE above, and for
# the same reason — it is a scheduler-only concern. Default: 3.
#
# This is the tick's throughput dial: a tick every two hours moving `batch`
# items processes `batch * 24 / 2` items per day, the number that decides
# whether the pipeline keeps pace with collection. Raising it does not require
# raising the unit's TimeoutStartSec= — `claude -p` runs once per worksheet,
# not once per item, so a bigger batch is still at most two agent calls (one
# for translate:prepare's worksheet, one for translate:align's). The ceiling is
# the timeout *inside* each of those calls: ClaudeCodeAgent caps one `claude -p`
# at 10 minutes (DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS), and a 30-item worksheet takes longer in
# that single call than a 3-item one. So raise this in steps and watch a tick's
# duration in the journal, rather than jumping to a number in one go.
#
# Must be a positive whole number. Unparseable or non-positive values are
# refused at startup rather than silently becoming `--limit 0`. Leave unset to
# keep the batch size of 3 every command already used.
HERALD_WATCH_BATCH=

# [OPTIONAL] Read only by `pnpm convert:tick`: how many approved *items* each
# conversion tick hands to `convert:prepare --limit`. Set on
# `deploy/herald-convert.service`, the second scheduler unit — the one that turns
# approved translations into channel variants every 30 minutes so 2차 검수 is
# already populated when a reviewer arrives. Default: 1.
#
# Items, not variants, and that is the whole reason this is a different number
# from HERALD_WATCH_BATCH above. One approved item fans out to every type it has
# not been converted for — up to six — and all of them are written by the SAME
# single `claude -p` call, under ClaudeCodeAgent's 10-minute cap. So this value
# multiplies by six before it reaches the only timeout that actually bounds a
# pass. Throughput is not the constraint: one item every 30 minutes is 48 a day,
# against a translate side whose own ceiling is 36 a day, of which only the ones a
# human approves in 1차 검수 reach this queue at all.
#
# Must be a positive whole number. Unparseable or non-positive values are refused
# at startup rather than silently becoming `--limit 0`. Leave unset to keep the
# default of 1.
HERALD_CONVERT_BATCH=

# [OPTIONAL] Read only by `pnpm doctor`: where the *deployed* checkout is, so that
# the report can say whether the steering config the schedulers run with is still
# the one this checkout holds. Leave unset unless this machine has two checkouts.
#
# Why a deployment has two: the systemd units run `pnpm <script>` out of their
# WorkingDirectory, so whatever is checked out there is what production runs, and
# `deploy/herald-deploy.sh` is the only thing that moves it — including the
# git-ignored steering config (glossary, style guide, locale, tm.json, the
# conversion prompts) it copies in at step 3. Between a `pnpm glossary add` here
# and the next deploy, the scheduler therefore keeps translating with the old
# glossary, and nothing anywhere says so. That gap is what the `Steering deploy
# sync` line in `pnpm doctor` reports.
#
# Normally you do NOT set this. Doctor asks systemd for
# `herald-watch.service`'s WorkingDirectory, which is the same directory the
# scheduler actually runs from, so a machine with the units installed already
# answers this question with nothing configured. Set it when a deploy checkout
# exists without those units — another box, a container — or to compare against a
# different tree than the one the units name. It wins over systemd when set, is
# resolved to an absolute path, and is only ever read: doctor never writes to the
# directory it points at. Where neither systemd nor this variable identifies a
# second checkout — every fresh clone, every CI run — the line reports "not
# applicable" and doctor's exit code is unaffected.
HERALD_DEPLOY_DIR=


# ═══ 2. Collection sources ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Needed in BOTH storage modes, but only for the sources you actually collect
# from. Collecting from X only? Leave the Lark block empty, and vice versa.

# [REQUIRED for `pnpm collect`] twitterapi.io API key (module A — X collection).
TWITTERAPI_IO_KEY=

# [OPTIONAL] Overrides the page cap (`DEFAULT_MAX_PAGES`, 50) that stops
# `fetchAuthoredTweets`/`fetchThread` from crawling forever. Read by exactly two
# commands — `pnpm collect` and `pnpm collect:reference` — and by nothing else:
# the gateway takes the cap as a constructor option, so `pnpm tm:measure`,
# `pnpm metrics:record`, `pnpm impressions:record` and `pnpm reconcile` build
# the same gateway and always get the default of 50, even with this exported in
# the shell. `pnpm watch` goes further and *refuses to start* while it is set
# (src/cli/watch.ts) — a tick has no legitimate reason to move the page cap.
#
# Set it only for a one-off hand-run backfill after a coverage GAP alert. The
# full procedure is docs/ko/team-runbook.md §4 "수집에 구멍이 생겼을 때": the cap
# is exactly why the GAP happened, so a plain re-run with the default re-truncates
# at the same point and recovers nothing — and the run also has to be pointed at
# the scheduler's own database and output root, or it "recovers" into the local
# Docker database while production keeps the hole. Must be a positive whole
# number; prefer setting it inline for that one command rather than exporting it.
HERALD_COLLECT_MAX_PAGES=

# [OPTIONAL] The KR official X account handle, no @ (e.g. 0xMantleKR). Used by
# `pnpm tm:measure`/`pnpm collect:reference` (translation-memory backfill), by
# `pnpm metrics:record` (the official account it measures), and by
# `pnpm x:reconcile` (the account it reads back to reconcile publish records),
# and by `pnpm x:link` (the account a named post must belong to before it will
# record one) — `--handle` overrides it in both. Default: 0xMantleKR.
# tests/config/envExampleReaders.test.ts asserts this list names every command
# that reads the variable, so it cannot silently fall behind a new reader.
REFERENCE_X_HANDLE=

# [REQUIRED for `pnpm collect-lark`, `pnpm lark:chats`, `pnpm lark:send`]
# Create a custom app in the Lark Developer Console, grant scope
# im:message.history:readonly (or im:message:readonly), and add the app/bot to
# each target group chat. See docs/ko/setup/lark.md.
LARK_APP_ID=
LARK_APP_SECRET=

# [REQUIRED for `pnpm collect-lark`] Comma-separated chat_id(s) to collect from
# (e.g. "Mantle News Announcement & MKT Task 2.0"). Find ids with
# `pnpm lark:chats`, which needs only the app credentials above — so leave this
# empty on first run. `pnpm lark:send` also works without it, but then --chat
# is mandatory rather than defaulting to the first id here.
LARK_CHAT_IDS=

# [OPTIONAL] API base domain. Default: https://open.larksuite.com (Larksuite
# international). Use https://open.feishu.cn for Feishu (China).
LARK_BASE_URL=


# ═══ 3. Cloud mode ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Only read when HERALD_STORAGE_MODE=cloud. In local mode, nine commands —
# drive:init, sheet:init, targets:list, history:record, impressions:record,
# metrics:record, kol-telegram:record, x:reconcile, x:link — skip with a message and
# exit 0, so you can leave the whole section empty. drive:publish is not one of them:
# in local mode it targets the filesystem instead of Drive, so it still runs, and it
# does not need anything from this section to do so.

# ── 3a. Google auth — [PICK ONE] method A or B ───────────────────────────────
# Detected automatically: a refresh token means OAuth, otherwise a key file
# means service account. Set this only to force one when both are present.
# Valid values: oauth | service_account
GOOGLE_AUTH_MODE=

# Method A — OAuth. Recommended, and the only option for a personal Gmail
# account: files are owned by YOU, so they use your storage quota.
# Create an OAuth client (type: Desktop app), set the id/secret, then run
# `pnpm google:auth` to mint the refresh token. See docs/ko/setup/google-drive.md.
# [REQUIRED for method A]
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# [OPTIONAL] Space-separated OAuth scopes requested by `pnpm google:auth`.
# Default: https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file (least privilege).
# Widen it only when you need to — the Sheet hub (3c) needs the extra scope:
#   GOOGLE_OAUTH_SCOPE="https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file https://www.googleapis.com/auth/spreadsheets"
# Changing this has no effect until you re-run `pnpm google:auth`; the token
# already carries the scopes it was minted with. `pnpm doctor --live` prints the
# scopes actually granted.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_SCOPE=

# Method B — Service account. A service account has NO storage quota of its own,
# so it can only upload into a Google Workspace **Shared Drive** (or via
# domain-wide delegation) — never a personal Drive. Leave empty for method A.
# [REQUIRED for method B] Path to the JSON key, e.g. keys/mantle-sa.json
GOOGLE_SA_KEY_FILE=

# ── 3b. Google Drive folders (subsystem D) ───────────────────────────────────
# [REQUIRED for `pnpm drive:publish`] Printed by `pnpm drive:init`, or copied
# from the Drive folder URL.
GDRIVE_REVIEW_FOLDER_ID=
GDRIVE_APPROVED_FOLDER_ID=

# [OPTIONAL] Used by `pnpm drive:init` only. Comma-separated team emails to
# share the folders with as editors. Empty means the folders stay private to you.
GDRIVE_SHARE_EMAILS=

# [OPTIONAL] Name of the parent folder `pnpm drive:init` creates.
# Default: "Mantle KR Herald". review/approved are nested inside it, and sharing
# the parent is what gives the team access (children inherit).
GDRIVE_PARENT_FOLDER_NAME=

# [OPTIONAL] Steering-config backup/share folder (pnpm config:push/pull). `config:push`
# provisions it and prints the id on first run if this is empty.
GDRIVE_CONFIG_FOLDER_ID=

# [OPTIONAL] Operational-state backup folder (pnpm state:push/pull) — everything under
# output/ that re-running the pipeline cannot reproduce: the reviewed Korean text
# (translations and conversions), per-room forks, the send ledgers, and the Drive sync
# ledger. `state:push` prints the exact list with row counts every run, so read that
# rather than counting here. It provisions the folder and prints the id on first run if
# this is empty. Keep it separate from the config folder: it records what THIS machine
# has already sent, so it is a personal backup, not a team share — restoring someone
# else's makes already-sent rooms read as never-sent.
GDRIVE_STATE_FOLDER_ID=

# [OPTIONAL] Sent-rendering archive folder for `pnpm send:channels`. Printed by
# `pnpm drive:init`. Unset means no Google archive copy is written (delivery
# still happens either way).
GDRIVE_SENT_FOLDER_ID=

# ── 3c. Google Sheet — data hub (§9a/§9b) + performance tracker ──────────────
# [REQUIRED for `pnpm sheet:init`, `pnpm targets:list`, `pnpm history:record`,
# `pnpm impressions:record`, `pnpm metrics:record`]
# Spreadsheet id (from the sheet URL, or printed by `pnpm sheet:init`). ONE
# workbook can hold every machine tab: `history` (history:record / send:channels)
# and `x-performance` (metrics:record) each auto-create their tab in whatever this
# id points at, so you can aim GSHEET_ID at your existing team workbook and skip
# sheet:init (which only pre-creates targets+history in a fresh sheet). `targets`
# for targets:list is the one tab you fill by hand. Reuses the Google auth above,
# but the token must carry the spreadsheets scope — see GOOGLE_OAUTH_SCOPE and
# docs/ko/setup/google-drive.md §공통 5.
GSHEET_ID=
# [OPTIONAL] A second workbook for QA. Its front tab is where anyone on the team
# files a request or a bug from using the dashboard; the rest track check status,
# open gaps and a dated change log. Nothing writes to it automatically — it is
# maintained by hand and linked from the dashboard header beside GSHEET_ID. Leave
# blank to hide that link. Same id format as above (from the sheet URL).
GSHEET_QA_ID=

# ── 3d. Lark Drive upload (subsystem D) ──────────────────────────────────────
# [REQUIRED for `pnpm drive:publish --target lark|both`] Reuses the Lark app
# from section 2 — add a drive scope to it. Folder tokens are the last path
# segment of the Lark drive folder URL. Google-only publishing (the default)
# does not need these.
LARK_DRIVE_REVIEW_FOLDER_TOKEN=
LARK_DRIVE_APPROVED_FOLDER_TOKEN=

# [OPTIONAL] Sent-rendering archive folder for `pnpm send:channels`. No
# auto-provisioning for Lark — create a `sent` folder by hand and paste its
# folder token (see the note above). Unset means no Lark archive copy is
# written (delivery still happens either way).
LARK_DRIVE_SENT_FOLDER_TOKEN=

# [OPTIONAL] The Lark workspace (tenant) origin — used only to build "open folder /
# open file in Lark" links in the review dashboard. It is the domain part of a Lark
# drive URL, e.g. https://<tenant>.larksuite.com (origin only, no path). Unset means the
# dashboard shows no Lark links; publishing is unaffected.
LARK_WORKSPACE_URL=


# ═══ 4. Channel delivery (§8) — pnpm send:channels ═══════════════════════════
# Needed in ANY storage mode — these are channel APIs, not cloud Drive. Only for
# the channels you actually send to.

# [REQUIRED for `pnpm send:channels --target telegram`] Bot token from BotFather;
# add the bot to each target room. See docs/ko/setup/channels.md. To also pin what it
# sends (--pin, or the dashboard's checkbox), the bot needs to be an admin of that
# room too — see channels.md T-2.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# [REQUIRED for `pnpm send:channels --target telegram`] One chat id per room, so
# two rooms on the same channel stay apart. Invite the bot, post any message in
# the room, then read result[].message.chat.id from
# https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates — supergroups and channels start
# with -100. A room left blank is skipped, and the dashboard names the missing
# variable. COMMUNITY = 맨틀 한국 커뮤니티, DEV = 맨틀 한국 데브방.
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_COMMUNITY=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_DEV=

# [REQUIRED for the `pnpm watch` scheduler's failure hook] Chat id that receives
# scheduler failures ONLY — nothing else ever posts here. Read by
# `deploy/herald-notify-failure.sh` (systemd's `OnFailure=` target for
# `herald-watch.service`), a separate unit from `pnpm watch` itself, straight
# out of this file the same way TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN above is. Left unset, the
# hook still exits 0 (a failing failure-handler would be its own loop) but sends
# nothing — exactly the silent-scheduler failure this whole feature exists to
# prevent, so set it before the timer (docs/ko/team-runbook.md) is installed.
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_OPS=

# [REQUIRED for `pnpm send:channels --target x`] Typefully v2 API key
# (typefully.com → Settings → API) and the social_set_id whose X account is
# @0xMantleKR (list them with GET /v2/social-sets). See docs/ko/setup/channels.md.
# X delivery is bounded by the plan's monthly publishing quota, not the API rate
# limit; run `pnpm doctor --live` to see the remaining count and reset date.
TYPEFULLY_API_KEY=
TYPEFULLY_SOCIAL_SET_ID=

# [OPTIONAL] Set to true only if the X account you publish to has X Premium. When true, x sends
# allow Premium long-form posts (up to 25,000 chars); when false (the default), they enforce the
# standard 280-weighted tweet limit (Korean/emoji count as 2 each).
X_PREMIUM=


# ═══ 5. Review dashboard — pnpm serve ════════════════════════════════════════

# [OPTIONAL] Port for the review dashboard. Default: 5757.
# The server binds localhost only.
PORT=

# [REQUIRED] The dashboard's single sign-in account. `pnpm serve` refuses to start
# without both — every route except POST /api/login now sits behind the session
# this account grants, so an unconfigured server has no safe way to serve at all.
#
# Generate the pair with `pnpm auth:hash` — it prompts for the password without
# echoing it and prints the two lines to paste here. Never put the password
# itself in this file; HERALD_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH is a scrypt hash, and knowing it
# does not get anyone in.
#
# The account is shared by the whole team — one login for everyone, not one per
# person. Rotating it (re-run `pnpm auth:hash`, replace both lines, restart) signs
# nobody out immediately (sessions are checked against HERALD_SESSION_SECRET below,
# not this pair), but the old password stops working right away — tell the team
# before you rotate, or the next person to sign back in will just see a refusal
# with no explanation. Two lockouts guard failed attempts, both tracked in the
# database so they survive a restart: five failures from one address lock that
# address for a minute, and fifty failures account-wide (the backstop against many
# addresses at once) lock the whole account for a minute. Both counts reset once an
# address (or the whole account) goes a minute without another failure, so this is a
# genuine 60-second window, not an ever-growing total — a single address cannot creep
# toward the fifty-failure backstop by serving its own one-minute lockout and trying
# again; only failures from several different addresses landing within the same
# window add up.
HERALD_AUTH_USERNAME=
HERALD_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH=

# [REQUIRED] Signs the dashboard's session cookie (HMAC-SHA256) once a login succeeds.
# Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32` — at least 32 characters, and anyone who
# has it can forge a valid session, so treat it like a password: never commit it, and
# rotate it (which signs everyone out at once) if it ever leaks.
HERALD_SESSION_SECRET=

# (HERALD_TRUST_PROXY and HERALD_TRUST_PROXY_HOPS used to sit here. They moved to §6:
# `pnpm serve` binds 127.0.0.1 with no proxy in front of it, so neither one changes
# anything here — they only ever mattered for the hosted deployment.)


# ═══ 6. Hosted deployment (Vercel) — api/[...path].ts ═══════════════════════
# The variables at the BOTTOM of this section are read only by the Vercel entry point —
# `pnpm serve` either ignores them or is unaffected by them. But this section is not the
# whole list: variables from every section above are required on the deployment too, and
# they are set as Vercel project environment variables (`vercel env add … production`),
# not in this file. The profile table at the top of this file is the summary; this is the
# account of how each one fails.
#
#   Refuses to start — you find out immediately, which is the good case:
#     DATABASE_URL, HERALD_DB_ENV (§1) · HERALD_AUTH_USERNAME,
#     HERALD_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH, HERALD_SESSION_SECRET (§5) ·
#     HERALD_STORAGE_MODE=cloud, HERALD_TRUST_PROXY=true, HERALD_DEPLOYMENT_ORIGIN
#     (all three below)
#
#   Starts fine and is quietly wrong — the case to actually watch:
#     §3 (Google auth + GDRIVE_REVIEW_FOLDER_ID/GDRIVE_APPROVED_FOLDER_ID) becomes
#       required as a consequence of that `cloud`, and it degrades silently too:
#       `createDeps` probes the Google config in a try/catch and simply omits the
#       google target when it throws. A deployment missing those credentials boots,
#       serves, and offers local-only publishing — the only hint is the storage-mode
#       badge's hover card in the dashboard header, which lists it as `키 없음`.
#     §2 and §4 are NOT needed to boot. §2 (collection) is a CLI job — the deployment
#       never collects; the keys only change what the dashboard's env panel reports.
#       §4 (channel delivery) is only reachable once HERALD_SENDS_ENABLED is on, but
#       set it in the same pass anyway: the panel reads it, and the alternative is
#       discovering a missing chat id at the moment of the first real send.
#
# The deploy runbook (DEPLOY.md) carries the copy-paste commands for all of this.

# ── 6a. Checking a deployment from your machine — NOT set on Vercel ──────────
# [OPTIONAL] The dashboard account `pnpm deploy:smoke` logs in as, so the check
# that proves a deployment actually works can run without a prompt — in CI, from
# a deploy script, or unattended. Unset, it prompts exactly as before.
#
# `pnpm creds:check` reads the same two and is the unattended case made literal:
# deploy/herald-creds.timer runs it daily at 06:23, so there is no tty to prompt
# on. Unset, that command refuses with exit 2 rather than hanging until systemd's
# TimeoutStartSec kills it.
#
# **LOCAL ONLY — never add these to the Vercel project.** Neither command runs
# inside the deployment; both run on your machine and knock on the deployed
# URL as a client. The deployment has no use for them, and a plaintext password
# in a deployment's environment is strictly worse than one in a local shell.
# The profile table above says the same thing in its own notation (`· · ·`).
# Note this is the password ITSELF, not the scrypt hash: HERALD_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH
# is the server proving who may log in, these are a client logging in.
#
# Set BOTH or NEITHER: with only one set the command refuses rather than prompting
# for the other, because a prompt on a runner with no tty hangs until the job times
# out and reads as a broken deployment instead of a missing secret.
HERALD_SMOKE_USERNAME=
HERALD_SMOKE_PASSWORD=

# ── 6b. Read by the Vercel entry point itself ────────────────────────────────
# [REQUIRED for the Vercel deployment — must be "true"; not read by `pnpm serve`] Trust
# the X-Forwarded-For header for the per-address login lockout (§5) instead of the raw
# socket address. A hosted deployment sits behind Vercel's own edge network, which is the
# only thing that can set that header truthfully there (nothing can reach the function
# without passing through it first) — and a Vercel Function has no raw socket the way
# `pnpm serve` does, so without this the per-address lockout has no address to key on at
# all, just the global 50-failure backstop: one stranger can hold the whole team's login
# out, silently. The Vercel entry point refuses to start rather than serve that degraded
# state — `assertTrustProxy` in `api/[...path].ts`.
#
# `pnpm serve` binds 127.0.0.1 with no proxy in front of it, so it reads the socket
# address and this changes nothing there. If you ever DO put a reverse proxy in front of
# a local server, it stops being optional: leaving it unset behind a proxy does not fail
# safe to "no per-address lockout" — every request then resolves to the proxy's own fixed
# address, so the lockout still fires, on one shared row for everyone behind it. That is
# the exact "one stranger locks out the team" failure the per-address layer exists to
# close, just keyed on a different address.
HERALD_TRUST_PROXY=

# [OPTIONAL] Only consulted when HERALD_TRUST_PROXY=true, so in practice only on the
# hosted deployment. Which entry in a comma-separated X-Forwarded-For chain, counted from
# the END, the trusted proxy is guaranteed to have set itself. Default: 1 — one proxy
# directly in front of this server, which is what Vercel is. Leave it unset unless you
# have added another hop. A value that is not a positive integer is rejected at startup
# by both entry points (`loadClientIpConfig`), even where the value would never be read:
# nonsense in the environment is a mistake worth reporting, not worth quietly ignoring.
HERALD_TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=

# [REQUIRED for the Vercel deployment — must be "cloud"] Stated in §1, repeated here
# because this is the one place its value (not just its presence) is constrained. §1's
# `local` is the correct starting point for `pnpm serve`; on the deployment it is refused
# at startup by `assertCloudStorage` in `api/[...path].ts`, because `local` there resolves
# publishing to this function's ephemeral filesystem: the upload reports success, the
# dashboard shows a published row, and every link is dead once the instance goes away.
# Set the value itself in §1 (or, for the deployment, as a Vercel environment variable).

# [REQUIRED for the Vercel deployment] The exact origin (scheme + host, no path) this
# deployment serves the dashboard from, e.g. https://<your-project>.vercel.app. The
# CSRF guard (`refusalReason`, shared with `pnpm serve`'s loopback-only check) refuses
# any state-changing request whose Origin is not this one. Never inferred and never
# defaulted permissively: Kyle's decision was the default *.vercel.app domain, so this
# deployment's own origin does not exist until it is deployed, and a CSRF allowlist
# that silently accepted everything because none was configured would be worse than no
# allowlist at all. Preview deployments are off by a separate decision (`vercel.json`'s
# git.deploymentEnabled: false) — there is exactly one origin to state.
HERALD_DEPLOYMENT_ORIGIN=

# [OPTIONAL] Opens POST /api/outlets/:id/:type/:outletId/send — the only route that can
# reach a live Telegram room or the brand's X account — on the hosted deployment. Unset
# (or anything other than the literal "true") means closed, which is how the hosted
# dashboard ships on its first deploy: 1차/2차 approval works, sends refuse with a
# Korean reason. `pnpm send:reconcile` and the dashboard's own [게시 확인] stay
# unaffected either way — reconcile only reads Typefully and writes urls onto rows that
# already exist. Not read by `pnpm serve`, which always sends, unaffected by this flag,
# exactly as it always has. Flip to "true" once the team trusts approvals — one
# variable, not new code.
HERALD_SENDS_ENABLED=
